Go-based malware (e.g., **Babuk ransomware variants**) often uses **Golang’s built-in compression and encryption** to obfuscate payloads. Static analysis with **Ghidra** reveals embedded strings, function calls, and import tables, but Go binaries have limited symbolic information due to compiler optimizations. To unpack the payload, a red-teamer would use **Frida** to hook Go runtime functions like `runtime·mallocgc`—used in memory allocation during unpacking. By injecting a Frida script that logs heap allocations and dumps memory regions, the analyst can extract the unpacked code. The Frida script may look like:
```javascript
Interceptor.attach(Module.getExportByName("libc.so.6", "malloc"), {
  onEnter: function(args) {
    console.log(`[+] Allocating chunk at ${args[0]} of size ${args[1]}`);
    // Dump memory if size > threshold
  }
});
```
Mitigation includes **adversary simulation via MITRE ATT&CK T1082**, and behavioral detection via EDR. Real analysis of Go malware was conducted in **2023 on BlackCat/ALPHV samples**.

